Maurice  Ravel,  Francis  Poulenc  and  Jean  Fran- 
gaix  have  much  less  in  common  than  one 
might  think.  Their  similarities  are  surface  ones 
and  have  more  to  do  with  matters  of  time  and 
place  than  with  anything  else. 

As  far  as  place  goes,  they  were  all  French.  As 
for  time,  they  are  closer  together  in  history  than 
the  casual  listener  might  suspect.  Ravel,  the  old¬ 
est,  was  born  in  1875;  Poulenc  in  1899;  Frangaix 
in  1912.  Thus  only  37  years  separates  the  oldest 
from  the  youngest.  Ravel,  though,  died  in  1937, 
Poulenc  in  1963,  while  Frangaix  is,  at  the  time 
of  writing  this,  very  much  alive. 

Musically,  the  differences  between  Ravel  and 
Poulenc  are  more  marked.  Ravel  was  a  superbly 
complete  musician,  a  gifted  pianist  and  a  bril¬ 
liant  orchestrator  -  one  of  the  greatest  masters 
of  instrumentation.  Poulenc  had  talent  and 
originality  but,  in  the  purely  technical  mastery 
of  his  craft,  he  was  hardly  in  Ravel's  class. 

Where  Poulenc  excelled  was  in  the  creation 
of  melody.  Virgil  Thomson  once  went  on  record 
as  saying,  "Poulenc  is  incontestably  the  greatest 
writer  of  melodies  in  our  time."  He  had  a 
healthy,  and  very  endearing,  strain  of  vulgarity, 
a  taste  for  ayral  ribaldry  and  the  contrast  be¬ 
tween  sublimity  and  absurdity.  If  he  had  lived 
ten  years  longer,  he  would  probably  have  been 
known  as  a  master  of  "camp,"  though  that  cur¬ 
rently  overused  word  does  little  justice  to  the 
totality  of  Poulenc's  art.  Francis  Poulenc  used 
this  flair  with  the  taste  of  a  connoisseur  and  the 
skill  of  a  natural  genius.  He  was  a  master  come¬ 
dian  roaming  through  the  halls  of  Olympus. 

Unless  one  falls  into  the  error  of  classing  him 
as  only  a  buffoon,  it  should  be  pointed  out 
that  he  also  wrote  the  opera  (or  monodrama) 
"La  Vpix  humaine,"  a  work  which  inspects  the 
emotions  of  a  woman  at  the  end  of  a  love  affair 
with  an  ynforgettable  combination  of  clinical 
exactness  and  compassionate  wisdom.  Near  the 
end  of  his  life  he  achieved  a  mastery  of  large 
musical  forms,  enabling  him  to  do  justice  to  a 
full-length  opera,  "Dialogues  des  Carmelites" 
(Angel  CL-3585),  a  drama  of  religious  and  per¬ 
sonal  faith  that  shows  every  sign  of  becoming  a 
repertory  piece  with  opera  houses  that  can  do 
it  vocal  and  dramatic  justice. 

Like  Poulenc,  Ravel  was  best  at  the  smaller 
forms  of  musical  work.  Unlike  Poulenc,  he  never 
emerged  with  a  work  as  formidable  in  scale  as 
"Dialogues  des  Carmelites"-  creating  instead 
two  short  masterpieces  in  the  operatic  genre, 
"L'Enfant  et  les  sortileges"  and  "L'Heure 
espagnole." 

He  was  an  excellent  teacher  and  may  have  had 
something  to  do  with  Poulenc's  musical  career 
when  he  urged  Poulenc  to  widen  his  acquaint¬ 
ance  with  music,  forget  about  the  avant  garde 
tendencies  of  the  time  and  increase  his  own 
technical  mastery. 

Ravel's  advice,  though,  had  little  serious  con¬ 
sequence.  Though  Poulenc  had  a  reputation  as 
a  composer  at  the  age  of  twenty,  he  never  had 
much  formal  training.  No  counterpoint,  no  or¬ 
chestration  at  all,  and  only  three  years  of  spas¬ 
modic  lessons  in  harmony. 

Not  so  in  the  case  of  Jean  Frangaix,  who  was 
extremely  fortunate  in  having  as  a  father  the 
former  director  of  the  Conservatory  of  Le  Mans 
at  which  he  studied  before  moving  on  to  the 
Paris  Conservatoire  and  winning  the  Prix  de 
Piano  in  1930.  Further  studies  with  Nadia  Bou¬ 
langer  expanded  his  already  formidable  tech¬ 
nical  mastery. 

As  a  creator  of  melodies  Frangaix  is,  perhaps, 
at  his  best,  though  his  melodies  are  neither  deep 
nor  serious.  Though  Poulenc's  melodic  writing 
can  have  a  tartly  sarcastic  surface  overlaying  a 
bittersweet,  melancholy  core,  Frangaix's  work 
tends  to  be  witty  but  not  very  self-revealing.  It 


