has  a  brittle  sophistication  that  is  none  the  less 
attractive  for  being  a  trifle  superficial. 

Frangaix's  musical  language  is,  for  the  most 
part,  classical  with  a  healthy  spiking  of  modern 
harmony  —  all  is  logical,  concise  and  agreeable, 
qualities  embodied  in  the  two  divertissements 
included  here. 

Ravel's  "Introduction  and  Allegro  for  Flute, 
Clarinet,  Harp  and  String  Quartet"  is  frequently 
referred  to  as  a  septet— which,  in  a  sense,  it  is. 
More  accurately.  It  is  a  concerto  for  harp  with 
accompaniment  for  flute,  clarinet  and  string 
quartet.  Commissioned  by  the  harpist  Michellne 
Kahn  in  1905,  the  work  came  close  to  being 
completely  lost. 

Ravel,  despite  his  meticulous  nature  in  things 
personal  and  musical,  was  notorious  for  mislay¬ 
ing  his  manuscripts.  When,  in  1905,  he  was  In¬ 
vited  to  go  yachting  with  friends,  he  at  first 
refused,  giving  as  his  reason  the  incomplete 
state  of  the  score  for  the  harp  concerto.  At  the 
last  minute,  though,  he  changed  his  mind, 
tucked  the  score  under  his  arm  and  dashed  off 
to  buy  some  new  shirts  for  the  trip.  He  bought 
the  shirts,  left  the  manuscript  in  the  shop,  and 
missed  the  boat  in  the  bargain.  He  seems  to 
have  been  more  concerned  about  the  missing 
boat  than  the  vanished  manuscript,  but  finally 
managed  to  locate  both.  The  shirtmaker  was 
an  amateur  musician,  had  taken  the  score  home 
with  him  and  only  let  Ravel  have  it  back  after  a 
long  argument.  Ironically,  Ravel's  letters  men¬ 
tion  the  shirts,  the  boat,  the  pleasures  of  the 
trip  —  but  not  a  word  about  the  concerto. 

It  was  a  source  of  concern  to  Poulenc  that  his 
technique  was  not  what  he  would  have  liked  it 
to  be.  When  he  achieved  something  of  which  he 
was  proud,  he  didn't  hesitate  to  express  his 
pleasure  with  it.  Of  the  "Trio  for  Oboe,  Bassoon 
and  Piano,"  he  wrote: 

"I  am  rather  fond  of  my  Trio  because  it  has  a 
transparent  sound  and  because  it  Is  well  bal¬ 
anced.  To  those  who  believe  me  careless  of 
form,  I  would  not  hesitate  to  reveal  my  secrets 
here:  the  first  movement  follows  the  plan  of  a 
Haydn  allegro,  and  the  final  rondo  is  in  the 
shape  of  the  scherzo  of  Saint-Saens'  second  pi¬ 
ano  concerto.  Ravel  always  advised  me  to  fol¬ 
low  this  sort  of  method,  just  as  he  often  did." 

—  Robert  T.  Jones 


